Q: PowerShell variable not outputting property A: <p>As explained in comments, <code>Format-List</code> and <code>Format-Table</code> are cmdlets specifically meant for console display formatting. Once you use them on your objects, they're transformed into different types and no longer have a <code>Name</code> or <code>Ip4Address</code> properties.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$out = Resolve-DnsName google.com | Format-List Name, Ip4Address
$out | ForEach-Object GetType

#    Namespace: Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.Internal.Format
# 
# Access        Modifiers           Name
# ------        ---------           ----
# internal      sealed class        FormatStartData : StartData
# internal      sealed class        GroupStartData : StartData
# internal      sealed class        FormatEntryData : PacketInfoData
# internal      sealed class        FormatEntryData : PacketInfoData
# internal      sealed class        GroupEndData : ControlInfoData
# internal      sealed class        FormatEndData : ControlInfoData
</code></pre>
<p>Just by removing that statement the issue should be resolved. You can also simplify a bit by piping <code>Get-Content</code> directly to <code>Resolve-DnsName</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$dnsresults = Get-Content 'C:\users\admin\Desktop\url.csv' | Resolve-DnsName

foreach ($result in $dnsresults) {
    if ($result.IP4Address -notcontains '172.67.214.35') {
        Write-Host &quot;$($IP.Name) is not in Cloudflare&quot;
    }
    else {
        Write-Host &quot;$($IP4.Name) is Proxied via Cloudflare&quot;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: The process cannot access the file because it is being used by another process. Jenkins Build A: <p>This error may occur due to the performance issue, in such cases we just need to use a loop like to avoid this, because, the current thread has to close before accessing by another process. </p>

<p>You can use the given method to access the file to attach with mail as follows:</p>

<pre><code>using(Attachment attpath = new Attachment(filepath))
{
    //your code to access the attached filepath
    mail.Attachment(attpath);
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I pop up Webkit&#39;s Web Inspector from my WebView object programmatically? A: <p>Here's an updated version of Alex's solution. It works with <code>WKWebView</code> instead of <code>WebView</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-objectivec prettyprint-override"><code>@interface _WKInspector : NSObject
    - (void)show;
    - (void)detach;
@end

void openWebInspector(WKWebView *webView) { /// Updated version of https://stackoverflow.com/a/19699592/10601702

    [webView.configuration.preferences setValue: @YES forKey: @&quot;developerExtrasEnabled&quot;]; /// Setting `webView.inspectable = YES` is not enough

    _WKInspector *inspector = [webView valueForKey: @&quot;_inspector&quot;];
    [inspector show];

    [inspector detach]; /// Doesn't do anything but you can detach manually and it will remember
}

</code></pre>
